<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Picardy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Picardy is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, centred on the Somme valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> as its main city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> among its towns. A French province under the old regime, it was an administrative region from the 1960s until 2016, when it merged with Nord-Pas-de-Calais into Hauts-de-France. Its open farmland was a frequent battleground, notably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-somme">Battle of the Somme</a> in 1916.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/picardy.jpg" alt="Picardy" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patricia.fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Picardie.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Picardy is a historical and cultural territory of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, never a duchy, county or principality, and its boundaries shifted over the centuries. The historical province stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> to Calais, through most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a> departments, the whole of the Somme and the west of the Pas-de-Calais, with the province of Artois separating it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-flanders">French Flanders</a>. The geographer Albert Demangeon described a Picard plain, a vast chalk plain stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>. Picardy runs from the sand beaches of the Somme estuary in the west to the forests and pastures of the Thiérache in the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 5th century the area formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a>, and in the feudal period it held the six countships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, Montreuil, Ponthieu, Amiénois, Vermandois and Laonnois. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>. The name was first used in the early 13th century for all lands where the Picard language was spoken, and it is said to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> pic, meaning pike. A Picard Nation of students existed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>. In 1358 the region was the centre of the Jacquerie peasant revolt.</p><p>From 1419 the Burgundian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> acquired the Picard counties, which Charles VII confirmed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1435">Congress of Arras</a> in 1435, and in 1477 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> occupied key towns there.</p><p>In 1557 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy">Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy</a>, invaded, ransacked St Quentin after a siege of seventeen days and burned Noyon. Napoleon I introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beet, and the sugar industry has remained prominent. Between September 1914 and August 1918 four major battles were fought along the Somme in northern Picardy, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-somme">Battle of the Somme</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 1 January 2016 Picardy has formed part of the Hauts-de-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> region, and its various definitions fall largely within it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> was the regional capital, and the main towns include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a>, Laon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, Compiègne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbeville">Abbeville</a>. The Picard language, one of the languages of France, is still spoken by a minority, and also in Artois and in parts of the Belgian province of Hainaut. The region holds some of the great Gothic cathedrals, among them Amiens Cathedral, the largest cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Saint-Pierre in Beauvais, which has the tallest transept of the Gothic period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Picardy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The setting contains a well-known <b>Picardy</b> third and a striking false relation.</blockquote><a href="/songs/coventry-carol">Coventry Carol</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ambiani held the lower and middle Somme in <b>Picardy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ambiani">Ambiani</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bellovaci lived in the modern Oise and <b>Picardy</b> around Beauvais.</blockquote><a href="/state/bellovaci">Bellovaci</a></li><li><blockquote>His family built up lands across <b>Picardy</b> and Champagne during the struggles between the Carolingian kings and the Robertians.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-vermandois">County of Vermandois</a></li><li><blockquote>Corvey was founded in 822 on the Weser near Höxter by monks from Corbie in <b>Picardy</b>, led by Adalard and Wala, cousins of Charlemagne, with the support of Louis the Pious.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-corvey">Prince-Abbey of Corvey</a></li></ul></section>
